    <Point><coordinates>-97.0304091213508,33.7170532261198,0</coordinates></Point>
  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Sivells Bend United Methodist Church</name>
    <styleUrl>#normal</styleUrl>
    <description><![CDATA[CR 437 at FM1201<br/><br/><b>Address:</b> Sivells Bend<br/><br/><b>Marker Text:</b> This congregation formed in 1868 as Sivells Bend Methodist Episcopal Church, South. Brothers William W. and Addison Y. Gunter conveyed two acres to church trustees E. L. Allen, WM. Midkiff and George Dillard in 1880. A large two-story church, school and masonic lodge burned in 1893. W. W. Gunter deeded the current site in 1910. The present frame church has had continuous services since its 1912 construction. During WWII, families taught Sunday school even without a supplied pastor. Later, Sivells Bend shared pastors with other Methodist churches, but has had its own pastors since 1950. Community involvement and regular worship shows a continuing focus on the true spirit of God.]]></description>
    <Point><coordinates>-97.230401,33.843868,0</coordinates></Point>
  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Spring Creek Cemetery</name>
    <styleUrl>#normal</styleUrl>
    <description><![CDATA[7 miles south of Gainesville on IH-35, then 2 miles west on Spring Creek Road<br/><br/><b>Address:</b> Gainesville<br/><br/><b>Marker Text:</b> The earliest burial on this site is that of infant Mary C. Underwood, who died in 1875. Another infant, identified only as "Newton's child," was interred on what became known as "Stranger's Row" in 1882. The following year, John P. and Sarah Jane (Ball) Barnhart donated this land to the newly formed Spring Creek Cemetery Association. Among the pioneers and their descendants interred here is Maleta Stapp Kleiser (1816-1902), whose father served in the Revolutionary War. Travis Anderson (1891-1918) died while in training during World War I, and Roy Speake, Jr. (1918-1944) died in the Allied invasion of Normandy during World War II. The Spring Creek Cemetery Foundation was organized in 1972 to maintain the historic graveyard. Spring Creek Cemetery is a chronicle of Cooke County. (2000)]]></description>
    <Point><coordinates>-97.2018730408623,33.5505831430682,0</coordinates></Point>
  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
